-- Q11: Internal Controls [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Inquiry of management about control design","Observation of the general control environment","Tests of controls such as reperformance or inspection","Review of the client''s organizational chart"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): AU-C 330 requires the auditor to perform tests of controls when planning to rely on their operating effectiveness. These tests include reperformance, inspection of evidence that the control operated, or a combination of procedures that go beyond inquiry alone.
Wrong (A): Inquiry of management is a risk assessment procedure but is insufficient by itself to test operating effectiveness of controls.
Wrong (B): Observing the general control environment does not constitute a test of whether specific controls operated effectively.
Wrong (D): Reviewing the organizational chart provides understanding of the entity but does not test control operation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11;

-- Q16: Audit Sampling [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It requires no statistical knowledge to implement","It stratifies the population by dollar amount automatically","It eliminates the need for understanding the client''s business","It produces results identical to a complete examination"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Monetary unit sampling (MUS) uses individual dollars as the sampling unit, so larger balances contain more sampling units and have a proportionally higher selection probability. This built-in stratification focuses the audit on high-dollar items without a separate stratification step (AU-C 530.A13).
Wrong (A): MUS does require statistical knowledge for proper application and evaluation of results.
Wrong (C): Understanding the client''s business is always required regardless of the sampling method chosen.
Wrong (D): No sampling method replicates the results of a 100% examination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 16;

-- Q20: Audit Reports [medium/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Unmodified opinion with no modifications needed","Qualified opinion for a material misstatement","Adverse opinion for pervasive misstatement effects","Disclaimer of opinion due to scope limitation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 705.08, when misstatements are material but not pervasive to the financial statements, a qualified opinion is appropriate. Misclassification of long-term debt as current is material to liquidity presentation but typically does not pervade the statements as a whole.
Wrong (A): An unmodified opinion is inappropriate because the financial statements contain a known material misstatement that management refuses to correct.
Wrong (C): An adverse opinion under AU-C 705.09 is reserved for misstatements that are both material and pervasive, affecting multiple elements or a substantial proportion of the statements.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer under AU-C 705.10 applies to scope limitations, not to identified misstatements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 20;

-- Q27: Quality Management [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Marketing and business development standards","A risk assessment process for identifying quality risks","Client profitability analysis and billing procedures","Staff compensation benchmarking and review"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): SQMS No. 1 requires firms to establish a risk-based approach to quality management, including a risk assessment process that identifies quality risks and designs responses to address them. The eight required components include governance, ethical requirements, acceptance and continuance, engagement performance, resources, information and communication, and monitoring.
Wrong (A): Marketing and business development are not components of quality management under SQMS No. 1.
Wrong (C): Client profitability analysis is a business decision, not a quality management requirement.
Wrong (D): Staff compensation benchmarking is a human resources function unrelated to the required quality management components.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 27;

-- Q97: Audit Planning [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It has no effect because each audit engagement is independent","The auditor should plan procedures to evaluate going concern","The auditor should decline the engagement entirely","The auditor should automatically reissue a going concern opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): A prior going concern qualification is a significant planning consideration under AU-C 570. The current auditor should assess whether the conditions that led to the previous qualification still exist and plan procedures to evaluate the entity''s current ability to continue as a going concern.
Wrong (A): While each audit is independent, prior findings inform current planning considerations and cannot be ignored.
Wrong (C): A prior going concern opinion does not require the successor auditor to decline the engagement.
Wrong (D): Each audit requires independent evaluation; a prior going concern does not predetermine the current opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 97;

-- Q101: Risk Assessment [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They move in the same direction as risk increases","Detection risk is always set higher than misstatement risk","They are completely unrelated concepts in the model","They have an inverse relationship under the audit risk model"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the audit risk model (AR = RMM x DR) in AU-C 315.A2, detection risk has an inverse relationship with the risk of material misstatement. When assessed RMM increases, the auditor must decrease acceptable detection risk by performing more effective or extensive substantive procedures (AU-C 330.07).
Wrong (A): A direct relationship would mean less testing when risk increases, contradicting the audit risk model.
Wrong (B): Detection risk is not always higher; its acceptable level depends on the assessed risk of material misstatement.
Wrong (C): The two risks are mathematically related through the audit risk model and are not independent.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 101;

-- Q104: Risk Assessment [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Confirming accounts receivable balances with third parties","Inquiring of management and performing analytical procedures","Recalculating depreciation expense for all fixed assets","Counting physical inventory on hand at the warehouse"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315.06, risk assessment procedures include inquiries of management and others, analytical procedures, and observation and inspection. These help the auditor understand the entity and identify risks of material misstatement at the financial statement and assertion levels.
Wrong (A): Confirming receivables is a substantive procedure performed in response to assessed risks under AU-C 330, not a risk assessment procedure.
Wrong (C): Recalculating depreciation is a substantive test of details, not a risk assessment procedure.
Wrong (D): Physical inventory counts are substantive procedures, not risk assessment procedures.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 104;

-- Q111: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Completeness of recorded receivables","Existence and accuracy of balances","Classification of account categories","Presentation and disclosure adequacy"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 505, accounts receivable confirmations primarily test existence (whether receivables actually exist) and accuracy (whether amounts are correct) by verifying recorded balances directly with customers.
Wrong (A): Confirmations sent to recorded customers test existence, not completeness. Testing completeness requires tracing from source documents to the ledger.
Wrong (C): Classification testing focuses on whether transactions are recorded in the proper accounts, which confirmations do not address.
Wrong (D): Presentation and disclosure testing examines financial statement display, not the underlying balance validity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 111;

-- Q112: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Comparing costs to market prices for NRV assessment","Observing the physical inventory count process","Sending confirmation requests to key suppliers","Tracing inventory purchases to the general ledger"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Valuation testing for inventory focuses on whether inventory is properly stated at the lower of cost or net realizable value under ASC 330. Comparing recorded costs to current market prices directly tests this assertion.
Wrong (B): Physical observation tests the existence assertion, not valuation, and has already been performed per the stem.
Wrong (C): Confirmations to suppliers primarily test accounts payable obligations, not inventory valuation.
Wrong (D): Tracing purchases to the general ledger tests the occurrence and accuracy of recorded transactions, not whether ending inventory is properly valued.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 112;

-- Q114: Audit Evidence [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To replace the need for performing other audit procedures","To confirm management''s responsibility and document representations","To serve as the primary basis for forming the audit opinion","To guarantee that the financial statements are fully accurate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 580.06, the management representation letter confirms management''s responsibility for the financial statements and formalizes oral representations made during the audit. It complements but does not replace other audit evidence.
Wrong (A): The letter supplements other evidence rather than replacing it; the auditor must still perform substantive procedures.
Wrong (C): The audit opinion is based on the totality of audit evidence gathered, not primarily on the representation letter.
Wrong (D): Management cannot guarantee accuracy; the letter acknowledges responsibility, not perfection.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 114;

-- Q116: Audit Sampling [medium/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Statistical sampling is always more effective than nonstatistical","Statistical uses random selection and probability-based evaluation","Nonstatistical sampling always requires much larger sample sizes","Statistical sampling can only be used for tests of controls"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 530.05, statistical sampling uses mathematical probability theory to determine sample sizes and evaluate results, requiring random selection. Nonstatistical sampling relies on auditor judgment for both sample size and evaluation (AU-C 530.A6).
Wrong (A): AU-C 530.A1 states both methods can provide sufficient appropriate evidence when properly designed; neither is inherently superior.
Wrong (C): Nonstatistical sampling does not inherently require larger samples; sample size depends on the auditor''s judgment and circumstances.
Wrong (D): Both statistical and nonstatistical sampling may be used for tests of controls and substantive tests.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 116;

-- Q117: Audit Sampling [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The actual rate of deviations discovered in the sample","The expected rate of deviations present in the population","The maximum deviation rate acceptable to still rely on controls","The probability that the sample results will be incorrect"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530, the tolerable rate is the maximum deviation rate from a prescribed control that the auditor will accept without modifying planned reliance. It is set during planning based on the degree of reliance on the control.
Wrong (A): The actual rate found in the sample is the sample deviation rate, which is a result of testing rather than a planning parameter.
Wrong (B): The expected population deviation rate is an estimate used in determining sample size, not the tolerable rate threshold.
Wrong (D): This describes sampling risk or confidence level, not the tolerable rate of deviation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 117;

-- Q118: Audit Sampling [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The population contains an unusually large number of items","The population is arranged in a completely random order","A cyclical pattern in the population aligns with the interval","The resulting sample size is smaller than initially planned"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530.A14-.A15, systematic selection uses a constant interval after a random start. If the population has a pattern coinciding with the sampling interval, the sample may be biased or unrepresentative. For example, if every 10th invoice is a month-end adjustment and the interval is 10, the sample systematically over- or under-represents those entries.
Wrong (A): A large population does not make systematic selection unreliable; the method works regardless of population size.
Wrong (B): A randomly ordered population actually helps systematic selection produce representative samples.
Wrong (D): Sample size is determined before selection and does not affect the reliability of the systematic method itself.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 118;

-- Q119: Audit Sampling [hard/L4]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The account balance is fairly stated as recorded","The sample does not support the balance and further procedures are needed","The account must be written down by the projected amount","The auditor should immediately issue a qualified opinion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 530.13, when projected misstatement exceeds tolerable misstatement, there is an unacceptably high risk the account is materially misstated. AU-C 530.14 requires the auditor to expand the sample, perform additional procedures, or request management to investigate and adjust.
Wrong (A): Accepting the balance when projected misstatement exceeds the tolerable threshold would be inappropriate and inconsistent with the sampling results.
Wrong (C): The projected misstatement is a statistical estimate, not necessarily the exact adjustment amount; further investigation is required first.
Wrong (D): The audit opinion is determined after evaluating all accumulated evidence, not based on a single sampling result.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 119;

-- Q122: Substantive Procedures [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Immediately inform management that an error exists","Conclude that the financial statements are materially misstated","Adjust the financial statements for the identified difference","Investigate the difference by performing additional procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 520.07, when analytical procedures identify a difference exceeding the acceptable threshold, the auditor must investigate by inquiring of management and obtaining corroborating evidence. AU-C 520.A18 states the investigation should determine whether the difference has a plausible, supported explanation.
Wrong (A): Automatically assuming the difference is an error without investigation is inappropriate; the difference may have a valid explanation.
Wrong (B): Concluding material misstatement based solely on an analytical procedure difference without further investigation is premature.
Wrong (C): Proposing an adjustment without first understanding the cause of the difference would be premature and unsupported.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 122;

-- Q123: Substantive Procedures [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Send confirmations to vendors with large recorded balances","Review the aged accounts payable trial balance for anomalies","Vouch recorded payables to the corresponding receiving reports","Examine post-year-end disbursements and trace to year-end payables"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 330.A47, testing completeness of accounts payable requires searching for unrecorded obligations. Examining cash disbursements after year-end is most effective because subsequent payments may relate to liabilities existing at the balance sheet date but not recorded (AU-C 500.A22).
Wrong (A): Confirming recorded balances tests existence and accuracy of amounts already in the records, not completeness.
Wrong (B): Reviewing the aged trial balance only examines recorded payables and would not reveal unrecorded liabilities.
Wrong (C): Vouching recorded payables tests existence and accuracy of items already recorded, not omitted obligations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 123;

-- Q126: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To communicate a scope limitation imposed by management","To report an uncorrected misstatement in the financial statements","To disclose a disagreement with management about accounting policy","To highlight a matter properly presented that is fundamental to users"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 706, an emphasis-of-matter paragraph draws attention to matters already properly presented or disclosed in the financial statements that the auditor believes are fundamental to users'' understanding, such as a significant accounting principle change or going concern uncertainty.
Wrong (A): Scope limitations affect the opinion itself and lead to qualified opinions or disclaimers, not emphasis-of-matter paragraphs.
Wrong (B): Uncorrected misstatements lead to opinion modifications (qualified or adverse), not emphasis-of-matter paragraphs.
Wrong (C): Disagreements with management about accounting policies may result in opinion modifications, not emphasis-of-matter paragraphs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 126;

-- Q129: Review/Compilation Engagements [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The accountant did not audit or review and provides no assurance","The accountant obtained reasonable assurance about the statements","Management is solely responsible for detecting all fraud","The financial statements are presented fairly in all material respects"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 80.21, the standard compilation report must state that the accountant did not audit or review the financial statements and therefore does not express an opinion or provide any assurance. This clearly communicates the limited nature of the engagement.
Wrong (B): Reasonable assurance is audit-level language; a compilation provides no assurance whatsoever on the financial statements.
Wrong (C): While management has responsibility for the statements, the compilation report does not state that management is solely responsible for detecting fraud.
Wrong (D): "Presented fairly in all material respects" is audit opinion language and is not included in a compilation report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 129;

-- Q130: Review/Compilation Engagements [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the anomaly since reviews do not require investigation","Inquire of management and consider whether additional procedures apply","Issue a qualified review report based on the analytical finding","Withdraw from the engagement due to the identified discrepancy"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 90.28, when analytical procedures reveal unusual or unexpected relationships, the accountant should inquire of management to obtain explanations and corroborating information. AR-C 90.29 requires additional procedures if management''s response does not adequately address the concern.
Wrong (A): Ignoring the matter would violate the accountant''s responsibility to obtain limited assurance under SSARS.
Wrong (C): Issuing a qualified conclusion based solely on one analytical anomaly without first inquiring of management is premature.
Wrong (D): Withdrawal is not warranted merely because an analytical procedure identified an unusual item requiring inquiry.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 130;

-- Q132: Review/Compilation Engagements [hard/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A preparation requires no report; a compilation requires one","There is no difference between the two engagement types","A compilation does not require understanding the client''s business","A preparation engagement provides a level of limited assurance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 70.05, a preparation engagement does not require a report; instead, each page must include a legend stating no assurance is provided. Under AR-C 80.20, a compilation requires the accountant to issue a written report. Neither engagement provides assurance.
Wrong (B): They are distinct services with different reporting and documentation requirements under SSARS.
Wrong (C): Both engagements require the accountant to have an understanding of the client''s industry and business.
Wrong (D): A preparation engagement provides no assurance at all, not limited assurance; limited assurance is provided in a review engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 132;

-- Q134: Attestation Engagements [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["GAAS for financial statement audits exclusively","AR-C standards for compilations and review engagements","SSAE attestation standards requiring an opinion on compliance","Government Auditing Standards (Yellow Book) exclusively"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): An examination of compliance is an attestation engagement governed by SSAE standards (AT-C sections). The CPA obtains sufficient appropriate evidence to express an opinion on whether the entity complied, in all material respects, with the specified requirements.
Wrong (A): GAAS governs audits of historical financial statements, not compliance examinations under attestation standards.
Wrong (B): AR-C standards govern compilations and reviews of financial statements, not attestation engagements.
Wrong (D): Government Auditing Standards may apply in addition to SSAE but do not exclusively govern compliance examinations performed by CPAs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 134;

-- Q136: Quality Management [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Taking overall responsibility for quality on the engagement","Setting the firm''s overall quality management policies","Approving the firm''s human resources and staffing policies","Conducting firm-wide inspections of all completed engagements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SAS No. 146, the engagement partner takes overall responsibility for managing and achieving quality on the audit engagement, including direction and supervision of the team, review of work, and ensuring consultation on difficult issues.
Wrong (B): Setting firm-wide quality management policies is a firm-level leadership responsibility, not an engagement partner duty.
Wrong (C): Approving human resources policies is a firm management function, not a responsibility of the engagement partner on a specific audit.
Wrong (D): Firm-wide inspection programs are monitoring activities managed at the firm level, not by individual engagement partners.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 136;

-- Q143: Professional Responsibilities [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["To assess individual CPA performance on the CPA exam","To evaluate firm compliance with applicable professional standards","To establish tax filing requirements for all CPA firms","To discipline individual CPAs for specific ethical violations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): The AICPA peer review program evaluates whether a CPA firm''s system of quality control is designed and operating in compliance with applicable professional standards. Peer reviews are firm-level evaluations covering audit, attest, and accounting engagements.
Wrong (A): Peer review examines firm-level quality control systems, not individual CPA exam performance.
Wrong (C): Tax filing requirements for firms are established by the IRS and state agencies, not the peer review program.
Wrong (D): Disciplinary action for ethical violations is handled by the AICPA Professional Ethics Division or state boards, not peer review.$EXPL$
